{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"SCOP 1.75 classified PDB 1acc chain A as one unsplit domain, d1acca_ (SUNID 43828; chain specification A:), in family f.11.1.1, superfamily f.11.1, and fold f.11, all named “Anthrax protective antigen.” This definition persisted through SCOPe 2.03. In SCOPe 2.04 it was replaced by two domains, and stable SCOPe 2.08 retains that split: the N-terminal d1acca2 (SUNID 238522), A:14-225, is family b.179.1.1 “PA14,” superfamily b.179.1 “PA14-like,” fold b.179; the C-terminal d1acca1 (SUNID 238521), A:226-735, is family f.60.1.1 and superfamily f.60.1 “Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like,” fold f.60. The listed newer spans leave residues 1-13 outside these classified domains.

The revision illustrates that a polypeptide chain is not necessarily one evolutionary unit: separately classifiable modules can be joined in one chain and acquire different evolutionary relationships. Each new domain has family- and superfamily-level homology implications within its own lineage. Because the two modules are assigned even to different SCOPe classes and superfamilies, SCOPe makes no superfamily-level common-ancestry assertion between them; this does not by itself prove independent origin, convergence, or absolute nonhomology. The retrieved classification records establish the changed boundaries and placements but do not expose the structural alignment, sequence analysis, literature argument, or curator reasoning that motivated the change.

Homology searches should therefore use A:14-225 and A:226-735 as separate queries or interpret full-chain hits by domain coverage. A hit confined to one segment supports a relationship only for that module—not homology of the entire chain, equivalence to legacy d1acca_, or transfer of the complete anthrax protective-antigen/toxin function to the matched protein. Whole-chain searches can obscure a remote domain match or produce misleading partial alignments across this junction. Stronger claims about boundaries, evolutionary origin, or function require domain-resolved sequence/profile evidence, structural alignment with residue coverage and conserved topology, compatible domain architecture, and—where function is transferred—ligand-bound or biochemical evidence; phylogenetic and literature support may also be necessary.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.03/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.04/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents"]}]}
